<section id="hub-diagram" class="mx-auto mt-8 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Understanding the Right Triangle Diagram</h2>
  <p>
    Every diagram and formula on this page follows one fixed convention. Learn it once and every
    label on the site reads the same way: vertices are <em>A</em>, <em>B</em>, and <em>C</em>, with the
    right angle always at <em>C</em>. Side <em>a</em> lies opposite angle <em>A</em>, side <em>b</em> lies
    opposite angle <em>B</em>, and side <em>c</em>, the hypotenuse, lies opposite the right angle. Because
    the angles of a triangle sum to 180°, the two acute angles always satisfy <span class="math-textbook" set:html={tex('A + B = 90^\\circ')} />.
  </p>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src="/images/hub/hub-parts.svg" alt="Labeled right triangle ABC with right angle at C, legs a and b, hypotenuse c, acute angles A and B, altitude h to the hypotenuse, and projection segments p and q" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">The standard labeling used across this page: <em>a</em> opposite <em>A</em>, <em>b</em> opposite <em>B</em>, hypotenuse <em>c</em> opposite the right angle at <em>C</em>.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <h3>Parts of a Right Triangle</h3>
  <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto">
    <table class="w-full min-w-[420px] border-collapse text-sm">
      <thead>
        <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/30 text-left">
          <th class="py-2 pr-4 font-semibold">Symbol</th>
          <th class="py-2 font-semibold">Meaning</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/15"><td class="py-2 pr-4 font-mono"><em>a</em></td><td class="py-2">Leg opposite angle <em>A</em></td></tr>
        <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/15"><td class="py-2 pr-4 font-mono"><em>b</em></td><td class="py-2">Leg opposite angle <em>B</em></td></tr>
        <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/15"><td class="py-2 pr-4 font-mono"><em>c</em></td><td class="py-2">Hypotenuse, opposite the right angle</td></tr>
        <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/15"><td class="py-2 pr-4 font-mono"><em>A</em>, <em>B</em></td><td class="py-2">The two acute angles (each between 0° and 90°)</td></tr>
        <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/15"><td class="py-2 pr-4 font-mono"><em>C</em></td><td class="py-2">The right angle, exactly 90°</td></tr>
        <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/15"><td class="py-2 pr-4 font-mono"><em>h</em></td><td class="py-2">Altitude: perpendicular distance from <em>C</em> to the hypotenuse</td></tr>
        <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/15"><td class="py-2 pr-4 font-mono"><em>p</em>, <em>q</em></td><td class="py-2">Projections of legs <em>a</em> and <em>b</em> onto the hypotenuse</td></tr>
        <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/15"><td class="py-2 pr-4 font-mono"><em>r</em></td><td class="py-2">Inradius: radius of the circle tangent to all three sides</td></tr>
        <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/15"><td class="py-2 pr-4 font-mono"><em>R</em></td><td class="py-2">Circumradius: radius of the circle passing through all three vertices</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="py-2 pr-4 font-mono"><em>s</em></td><td class="py-2">Semiperimeter: half of the perimeter, <em>s</em> = (<em>a</em> + <em>b</em> + <em>c</em>) / 2</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    When you enter valid measurements in the calculator above, its live diagram redraws the triangle to
    scale with your values and labels each computed side and angle, so the picture always matches the numbers.
  </p>
</section>

<section id="hub-how-to" class="mx-auto mt-8 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Use the Right Triangle Calculator</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Identify your known measurements.</strong> You need two independent values with at least one side: two sides, or one side plus one acute angle.</li>
    <li><strong>Enter each value in its matching field.</strong> Side <em>a</em> is opposite angle <em>A</em>, side <em>b</em> is opposite angle <em>B</em>, and <em>c</em> is always the hypotenuse. Angles are entered in degrees.</li>
    <li><strong>Run the calculation.</strong> The solver finds every missing side and angle, plus area, perimeter, altitude, projections, inradius, and circumradius.</li>
    <li><strong>Examine the diagram.</strong> The triangle redraws to the proportions of your solution, with each measurement labeled at its position.</li>
    <li><strong>Review the solution steps.</strong> Each result shows the formula used, the substituted values, and the final answer, so you can follow the arithmetic.</li>
    <li><strong>Verify the result.</strong> Confirm the hypotenuse is the longest side and the two acute angles add to 90°. The verification checklist below automates these checks.</li>
  </ol>
  <h3>Input Rules the Calculator Enforces</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Sides must be positive.</strong> Entering <em>a</em> = −3 is rejected; lengths cannot be negative or zero.</li>
    <li><strong>The hypotenuse must be the longest side.</strong> Entering <em>c</em> = 5 with <em>a</em> = 6 is impossible, because no leg can exceed the hypotenuse.</li>
    <li><strong>Acute angles are strictly between 0° and 90°.</strong> An entry of <em>A</em> = 0° or <em>A</em> = 90° describes a degenerate triangle, not a right triangle.</li>
    <li><strong>Two angles alone are not enough.</strong> <em>A</em> = 30° and <em>B</em> = 60° describe infinitely many similar triangles; without a side, the size is undetermined.</li>
    <li><strong>Angles A and B must be complementary.</strong> Entering <em>A</em> = 40° and <em>B</em> = 60° contradicts <em>A</em> + <em>B</em> = 90° and is rejected.</li>
    <li><strong>Measurements must be independent.</strong> Entering <em>a</em> = 3 and <em>c</em> = 5 determines the triangle; entering <em>a</em> = 3 twice does not add information.</li>
    <li><strong>Keep length units consistent.</strong> All side inputs share one unit selector; mixing meters and centimeters in one calculation gives a wrong answer.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section id="hub-fundamentals" class="mx-auto mt-8 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>What Is a Right Triangle?</h2>
  <p>
    A right triangle is a triangle containing one 90° angle, the right angle. The 90° angle fixes the
    shape more rigidly than any other single angle: once it is set, the remaining two angles must share the
    leftover 90°, which forces both to be acute (each strictly less than 90°). The side opposite the right
    angle is the hypotenuse, always the longest side; the other two sides are the legs, and they meet
    perpendicularly at the right-angle vertex.
  </p>
  <p>
    This perpendicularity is what makes right triangles the workhorse of measurement. Any rectangle splits
    along its diagonal into two congruent right triangles, so every rectangular length, from a floor plan to
    a ramp, hides a right triangle. A right triangle is uniquely determined by two independent measurements
    that include at least one side, which is exactly why the calculator asks for two values: that is the
    minimum information from which the other four sides and angles can be derived.
  </p>
  <p>
    The labels <em>opposite</em> and <em>adjacent</em> always refer to a chosen acute angle. Pick angle
    <em>A</em>: the leg across from it (<em>a</em>) is opposite, and the leg forming the angle (<em>b</em>) is
    adjacent. Pick angle <em>B</em> instead and the two legs trade roles. The interactive diagram below lets
    you switch between the two viewpoints.
  </p>
  <h3>Why Is the Hypotenuse the Longest Side?</h3>
  <p>
    The Pythagorean theorem states <span class="math-textbook" set:html={tex('a^2 + b^2 = c^2')} />. Because
    side lengths are positive, <span class="math-textbook" set:html={tex('a^2 + b^2 > a^2')} /> and
    <span class="math-textbook" set:html={tex('a^2 + b^2 > b^2')} />, so
    <span class="math-textbook" set:html={tex('c^2 > a^2')} /> and <span class="math-textbook" set:html={tex('c^2 > b^2')} />.
    Taking square roots of positive numbers preserves the inequality, giving
    <span class="math-textbook" set:html={tex('c > a')} /> and <span class="math-textbook" set:html={tex('c > b')} />.
    In words: adding the square of the other leg can only lengthen the hypotenuse, so the hypotenuse must
    exceed either leg. Any input that violates this, such as <em>c</em> = 5 with <em>a</em> = 6, cannot be a
    right triangle.
  </p>
</section>

<section id="hub-formulas" class="mx-auto mt-8 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Right Triangle Formulas Explained</h2>
  <p>
    Every result on this page comes from the formulas below. Each card states the formula, when to use it,
    and which calculator on this site handles that exact task.
  </p>

  <h3>Pythagorean Theorem</h3>
  <div class="not-prose my-4 rounded-xl border border-outline-variant/25 bg-surface-container-lowest/60 p-5 text-center">
    <p class="math-textbook text-xl" set:html={texBlock('a^2 + b^2 = c^2')} />
  </div>
  <p>Use the rearranged form that isolates the unknown side:</p>
  <div class="not-prose my-4 grid gap-3 sm:grid-cols-3">
    <div class="rounded-xl border border-outline-variant/25 bg-surface-container-lowest/60 p-4 text-center">
      <p class="text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Find the hypotenuse</p>
      <p class="math-textbook mt-1 text-lg" set:html={tex('c = \\sqrt{a^2 + b^2}')} />
    </div>
    <div class="rounded-xl border border-outline-variant/25 bg-surface-container-lowest/60 p-4 text-center">
      <p class="text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Find leg <em>a</em></p>
      <p class="math-textbook mt-1 text-lg" set:html={tex('a = \\sqrt{c^2 - b^2}')} />
    </div>
    <div class="rounded-xl border border-outline-variant/25 bg-surface-container-lowest/60 p-4 text-center">
      <p class="text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Find leg <em>b</em></p>
      <p class="math-textbook mt-1 text-lg" set:html={tex('b = \\sqrt{c^2 - a^2}')} />
    </div>
  </div>
  <p>
    Use the first form when both legs are known, and the other two when the hypotenuse and one leg are known.
    The value under the square root must be positive; a negative value means the supposed hypotenuse is not
    longer than the known leg. See the <a href="/right-triangle-side-calculator/">Right Triangle Side Calculator</a>
    for focused side solving.
  </p>

  <h3>Trigonometric Formulas</h3>
  <p>Relative to angle <em>A</em>, the three primary ratios are:</p>
  <div class="not-prose my-4 rounded-xl border border-outline-variant/25 bg-surface-container-lowest/60 p-5 text-center">
    <p class="math-textbook text-lg" set:html={texBlock('\\sin(A) = \\frac{a}{c} \\qquad \\cos(A) = \\frac{b}{c} \\qquad \\tan(A) = \\frac{a}{b}')} />
  </div>
  <p>
    <strong>SOH–CAH–TOA</strong> is the memory aid: <strong>S</strong>ine = <strong>O</strong>pposite over
    <strong>H</strong>ypotenuse, <strong>C</strong>osine = <strong>A</strong>dacent over <strong>H</strong>ypotenuse,
    <strong>T</strong>angent = <strong>O</strong>pposite over <strong>A</strong>dacent. To go the other direction,
    from sides to an angle, use the inverse functions:
  </p>
  <div class="not-prose my-4 rounded-xl border border-outline-variant/25 bg-surface-container-lowest/60 p-5 text-center">
    <p class="math-textbook text-lg" set:html={texBlock('A = \\arcsin\\frac{a}{c} \\qquad A = \\arccos\\frac{b}{c} \\qquad A = \\arctan\\frac{a}{b} \\qquad B = 90^\\circ - A')} />
  </div>
  <p>
    Pick the inverse formula whose fraction uses the sides you actually know, then subtract from 90° for the
    remaining angle. The <a href="/right-triangle-angle-calculator/">Right Triangle Angle Calculator</a> and the
    <a href="/right-triangle-trig-ratios-calculator/">Trigonometric Ratios Calculator</a> cover these tasks in depth.
  </p>

  <h3>Area and Perimeter</h3>
  <div class="not-prose my-4 rounded-xl border border-outline-variant/25 bg-surface-container-lowest/60 p-5 text-center">
    <p class="math-textbook text-lg" set:html={texBlock('K = \\frac{a \\times b}{2} \\qquad P = a + b + c \\qquad s = \\frac{P}{2}')} />
  </div>
  <p>
    The legs are perpendicular, so they act directly as base and height: no altitude computation is needed for
    the area. The semiperimeter <em>s</em> is half the perimeter and appears in the inradius formula and in
    Heron's formula. Try the <a href="/right-triangle-area-calculator/">Right Triangle Area Calculator</a> for
    area-first problems.
  </p>

  <h3>Altitude to the Hypotenuse</h3>
  <div class="not-prose my-4 rounded-xl border border-outline-variant/25 bg-surface-container-lowest/60 p-5 text-center">
    <p class="math-textbook text-lg" set:html={texBlock('h = \\frac{a \\times b}{c}')} />
  </div>
  <p>
    The altitude <em>h</em> is the perpendicular distance from the right-angle vertex <em>C</em> to the
    hypotenuse. It follows from equating the two expressions for the area:
    <span class="math-textbook" set:html={tex('\\frac{a \\times b}{2} = \\frac{h \\times c}{2}')} />, then solving
    for <em>h</em>. Dedicated tool: <a href="/right-triangle-altitude-calculator/">Height (Altitude) Calculator</a>.
  </p>

  <h3>Hypotenuse Projections</h3>
  <div class="not-prose my-4 rounded-xl border border-outline-variant/25 bg-surface-container-lowest/60 p-5 text-center">
    <p class="math-textbook text-lg" set:html={texBlock('p = \\frac{a^2}{c} \\qquad q = \\frac{b^2}{c} \\qquad p + q = c \\qquad h^2 = p \\times q')} />
  </div>
  <p>
    The altitude lands at point <em>D</em> on the hypotenuse, splitting it into segment <em>p</em> (adjacent to
    leg <em>a</em>, between <em>B</em> and <em>D</em>) and segment <em>q</em> (adjacent to leg <em>b</em>, between
    <em>D</em> and <em>A</em>). The relation <span class="math-textbook" set:html={tex('h^2 = p \\times q')} /> is the
    geometric mean: the altitude is the geometric mean of the two projections. Dedicated tool:
    <a href="/right-triangle-projection-calculator/">Projection and Segment Calculator</a>.
  </p>

  <h3>Inradius and Circumradius</h3>
  <div class="not-prose my-4 rounded-xl border border-outline-variant/25 bg-surface-container-lowest/60 p-5 text-center">
    <p class="math-textbook text-lg" set:html={texBlock('r = \\frac{a + b - c}{2} \\qquad R = \\frac{c}{2}')} />
  </div>
  <p>
    The <strong>incircle</strong> is the circle tangent to all three sides; its center (the incenter) sits at
    distance <em>r</em> from each side. The <strong>circumcircle</strong> passes through all three vertices; for
    a right triangle its center is the midpoint of the hypotenuse, so the hypotenuse is a diameter and
    <span class="math-textbook" set:html={tex('R = c/2')} /> exactly. Dedicated tool:
    <a href="/right-triangle-radius-calculator/">Inradius and Circumradius Calculator</a>.
  </p>

  <h3>Formula Quick Reference</h3>
  <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto">
    <table class="w-full min-w-[640px] border-collapse text-sm">
      <thead>
        <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/30 text-left">
          <th class="py-2 pr-3 font-semibold">Measurement</th>
          <th class="py-2 pr-3 font-semibold">Formula</th>
          <th class="py-2 pr-3 font-semibold">Known values</th>
          <th class="py-2 font-semibold">Calculator</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/15"><td class="py-2 pr-3">Hypotenuse</td><td class="py-2 pr-3 math-textbook" set:html={tex('c = \\sqrt{a^2 + b^2}')} /><td class="py-2 pr-3">legs <em>a</em>, <em>b</em></td><td class="py-2"><a href="/right-triangle-side-calculator/">Side</a></td></tr>
        <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/15"><td class="py-2 pr-3">Leg <em>a</em></td><td class="py-2 pr-3 math-textbook" set:html={tex('a = \\sqrt{c^2 - b^2}')} /><td class="py-2 pr-3"><em>c</em> &gt; <em>b</em></td><td class="py-2"><a href="/right-triangle-side-calculator/">Side</a></td></tr>
        <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/15"><td class="py-2 pr-3">Leg <em>a</em> from angle</td><td class="py-2 pr-3 math-textbook" set:html={tex('a = c\\sin A')} /><td class="py-2 pr-3"><em>c</em>, acute <em>A</em></td><td class="py-2"><a href="/right-triangle-side-from-angle-calculator/">Side from Angle</a></td></tr>
        <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/15"><td class="py-2 pr-3">Angle <em>A</em></td><td class="py-2 pr-3 math-textbook" set:html={tex('A = \\arctan\\frac{a}{b}')} /><td class="py-2 pr-3">legs <em>a</em>, <em>b</em></td><td class="py-2"><a href="/right-triangle-angle-calculator/">Angle</a></td></tr>
        <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/15"><td class="py-2 pr-3">Trig ratios</td><td class="py-2 pr-3 math-textbook" set:html={tex('\\sin A = a/c')} /><td class="py-2 pr-3">any two of <em>a</em>, <em>b</em>, <em>c</em></td><td class="py-2"><a href="/right-triangle-trig-ratios-calculator/">Trig Ratios</a></td></tr>
        <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/15"><td class="py-2 pr-3">Area</td><td class="py-2 pr-3 math-textbook" set:html={tex('K = ab/2')} /><td class="py-2 pr-3">legs <em>a</em>, <em>b</em></td><td class="py-2"><a href="/right-triangle-area-calculator/">Area</a></td></tr>
        <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/15"><td class="py-2 pr-3">Perimeter</td><td class="py-2 pr-3 math-textbook" set:html={tex('P = a+b+c')} /><td class="py-2 pr-3">all three sides</td><td class="py-2"><a href="/right-triangle-measurements-calculator/">Measurements</a></td></tr>
        <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/15"><td class="py-2 pr-3">Altitude</td><td class="py-2 pr-3 math-textbook" set:html={tex('h = ab/c')} /><td class="py-2 pr-3">valid triangle</td><td class="py-2"><a href="/right-triangle-altitude-calculator/">Altitude</a></td></tr>
        <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/15"><td class="py-2 pr-3">Projection <em>p</em></td><td class="py-2 pr-3 math-textbook" set:html={tex('p = a^2/c')} /><td class="py-2 pr-3"><em>c</em> &gt; <em>a</em></td><td class="py-2"><a href="/right-triangle-projection-calculator/">Projection</a></td></tr>
        <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/15"><td class="py-2 pr-3">Inradius</td><td class="py-2 pr-3 math-textbook" set:html={tex('r = (a+b-c)/2')} /><td class="py-2 pr-3">valid triangle</td><td class="py-2"><a href="/right-triangle-radius-calculator/">Radius</a></td></tr>
        <tr><td class="py-2 pr-3">Circumradius</td><td class="py-2 pr-3 math-textbook" set:html={tex('R = c/2')} /><td class="py-2 pr-3"><em>c</em> &gt; 0</td><td class="py-2"><a href="/right-triangle-radius-calculator/">Radius</a></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="hub-visualizations" class="mx-auto mt-8 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Seeing the Math: Key Diagrams</h2>
  <p>Four diagrams that make the central relationships visible. Each is drawn from the same 3-4-5 triangle used in the worked examples.</p>

  <h3>Diagram 1: The Pythagorean Theorem as Areas</h3>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src="/images/hub/hub-pythagoras-squares.svg" alt="3-4-5 right triangle with squares of area 9, 16, and 25 built on its three sides, showing 9 + 16 = 25" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-2xl" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">The square on leg <em>a</em> has area 9, on leg <em>b</em> area 16, and on the hypotenuse area 25. Since 9 + 16 = 25, the two smaller squares exactly fill the largest one.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    This picture is a proof for the illustrated triangle: the combined area of the squares on the legs equals
    the area of the square on the hypotenuse. The general theorem states this holds for <em>every</em> right
    triangle, which is why <span class="math-textbook" set:html={tex('a^2 + b^2 = c^2')} /> can be trusted as a
    calculation rule rather than a coincidence of the 3-4-5 example.
  </p>

  <h3>Diagram 2: Opposite, Adjacent, and Hypotenuse</h3>
  <p>Select an angle to see which leg is opposite and which is adjacent. The hypotenuse never changes.</p>
  <div class="not-prose my-6" data-opp-adj-widget>
    <div class="mb-3 flex gap-2" role="group" aria-label="Select angle">
      <button type="button" class="interactive-chip is-active" data-angle-btn="A" aria-pressed="true">Angle A</button>
      <button type="button" class="interactive-chip" data-angle-btn="B" aria-pressed="false">Angle B</button>
    </div>
    <div class="mx-auto w-full max-w-xl" data-opp-adj-svg set:html={inlineHubSvg('hub-opp-adj.svg')} />
    <p class="mt-3 rounded-xl border border-outline-variant/25 bg-surface-container-lowest/60 p-4 text-sm" data-opp-adj-text>
      <strong>Angle A selected:</strong> opposite = <em>a</em> = 3, adjacent = <em>b</em> = 4, hypotenuse = <em>c</em> = 5.
      sin(A) = 3/5, cos(A) = 4/5, tan(A) = 3/4.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Text version: with angle A, the side across the triangle (a = 3) is opposite and the side forming the angle (b = 4) is adjacent. With angle B, they swap: b = 4 is opposite and a = 3 is adjacent. The hypotenuse c = 5 stays the hypotenuse in both views.</p>
  </div>

  <h3>Diagram 3: Altitude and Projections</h3>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src="/images/hub/hub-altitude-projections.svg" alt="3-4-5 right triangle with altitude h = 2.4 from the right angle to the hypotenuse, splitting it into projections p = 1.8 and q = 3.2" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">The altitude <em>h</em> = 2.4 lands at <em>D</em>, creating projections <em>p</em> = 1.8 and <em>q</em> = 3.2 with <em>p</em> + <em>q</em> = 5 = <em>c</em>.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    The altitude divides the original triangle into two smaller triangles, and each of them is
    <strong>similar</strong> to the original: all three share the same three angles (36.87°, 53.13°, 90°).
    Similarity is why the projection formulas work, since corresponding sides of similar triangles stay in
    proportion: <span class="math-textbook" set:html={tex('a^2 = c \\times p')} />,
    <span class="math-textbook" set:html={tex('b^2 = c \\times q')} />, and
    <span class="math-textbook" set:html={tex('h^2 = p \\times q')} />. Check: 1.8 + 3.2 = 5, and
    2.4² = 5.76 = 1.8 × 3.2.
  </p>

  <h3>Diagram 4: Incircle and Circumcircle</h3>
  <p>Toggle each circle to compare them. The incircle fits inside; the circumcircle passes through all three vertices.</p>
  <div class="not-prose my-6" data-circles-widget>
    <div class="mb-3 flex flex-wrap gap-4 text-sm">
      <label class="inline-flex cursor-pointer items-center gap-2">
        <input type="checkbox" class="h-4 w-4 accent-[#4ade80]" data-circle-toggle="in" checked /> Show incircle
      </label>
      <label class="inline-flex cursor-pointer items-center gap-2">
        <input type="checkbox" class="h-4 w-4 accent-[#38bdf8]" data-circle-toggle="circum" /> Show circumcircle
      </label>
    </div>
    <div class="mx-auto w-full max-w-xl" data-circles-svg set:html={inlineHubSvg('hub-circles.svg')} />
    <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Text version: the incircle (green) touches all three sides with center 1 unit from each side and radius r = (3 + 4 − 5) / 2 = 1. The circumcircle (blue) passes through A, B, and C with its center at the midpoint of the hypotenuse and radius R = 5 / 2 = 2.5, exactly half the hypotenuse.</p>
  </div>
  <p>
    The two circles answer different questions. The <strong>incircle</strong> is the largest circle that fits
    inside the triangle, useful in packing and clearance problems. The <strong>circumcircle</strong> is the
    smallest circle containing the triangle; because the hypotenuse is its diameter, Thales' theorem guarantees
    that any triangle inscribed in a semicircle with the diameter as one side is a right triangle.
  </p>
</section>

<script is:inline>
  (() => {
    document.querySelectorAll('[data-opp-adj-widget]').forEach((widget) => {
      const svgHost = widget.querySelector('[data-opp-adj-svg]');
      const text = widget.querySelector('[data-opp-adj-text]');
      const buttons = Array.from(widget.querySelectorAll('[data-angle-btn]'));
      const setAngle = (angle) => {
        buttons.forEach((btn) => {
          const active = btn.getAttribute('data-angle-btn') === angle;
          btn.classList.toggle('is-active', active);
          btn.setAttribute('aria-pressed', active ? 'true' : 'false');
        });
        if (svgHost) {
          svgHost.querySelectorAll('[data-view]').forEach((g) => {
            g.style.display = g.getAttribute('data-view') === angle ? '' : 'none';
          });
        }
        if (text) {
          text.innerHTML = angle === 'A'
            ? '<strong>Angle A selected:</strong> opposite = <em>a</em> = 3, adjacent = <em>b</em> = 4, hypotenuse = <em>c</em> = 5. sin(A) = 3/5, cos(A) = 4/5, tan(A) = 3/4.'
            : '<strong>Angle B selected:</strong> opposite = <em>b</em> = 4, adjacent = <em>a</em> = 3, hypotenuse = <em>c</em> = 5. sin(B) = 4/5, cos(B) = 3/5, tan(B) = 4/3.';
        }
      };
      buttons.forEach((btn) => {
        btn.addEventListener('click', () => setAngle(btn.getAttribute('data-angle-btn') || 'A'));
      });
      setAngle('A');
    });

    document.querySelectorAll('[data-circles-widget]').forEach((widget) => {
      const svgHost = widget.querySelector('[data-circles-svg]');
      const toggles = Array.from(widget.querySelectorAll('[data-circle-toggle]'));
      const refresh = () => {
        const state = {};
        toggles.forEach((t) => { state[t.getAttribute('data-circle-toggle')] = t.checked; });
        if (svgHost) {
          svgHost.querySelectorAll('[data-circle]').forEach((g) => {
            g.style.display = state[g.getAttribute('data-circle')] ? '' : 'none';
          });
        }
      };
      toggles.forEach((t) => t.addEventListener('change', refresh));
      refresh();
    });
  })();
</script>

<section id="hub-examples" class="mx-auto mt-8 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Step-by-Step Worked Examples</h2>
  <p>
    Five complete solutions covering the input combinations the calculator accepts. Each example shows the
    formula, the substitution, every intermediate step, and a geometric check. Use the
    <strong>Try in Calculator</strong> button to load the example into the solver above and confirm the results.
  </p>

  <article class="not-prose my-6 rounded-xl border border-outline-variant/25 bg-surface-container-lowest/60 p-5">
    <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold">Example 1: Find the Hypotenuse from Two Legs</h3>
    <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-on-surface-variant"><strong>Given:</strong> <em>a</em> = 3 cm, <em>b</em> = 4 cm. <strong>Find:</strong> <em>c</em>, angles, and derived measurements.</p>
    <img src="/images/hub/hub-ex1-345.svg" alt="3-4-5 right triangle with labeled sides and angles 36.87 and 53.13 degrees" class="mx-auto my-4 h-auto w-full max-w-sm" loading="lazy" />
    <ol class="list-decimal space-y-1 pl-5 text-sm">
      <li>Formula: <span class="math-textbook" set:html={tex('c = \\sqrt{a^2 + b^2}')} /></li>
      <li>Substitution: <span class="math-textbook" set:html={tex('c = \\sqrt{3^2 + 4^2}')} /></li>
      <li>Simplify: <span class="math-textbook" set:html={tex('c = \\sqrt{9 + 16} = \\sqrt{25}')} /></li>
      <li>Answer: <strong><em>c</em> = 5 cm</strong></li>
      <li>Angles: <span class="math-textbook" set:html={tex('A = \\arctan(3/4) \\approx 36.87^\\circ')} />, <span class="math-textbook" set:html={tex('B = 90^\\circ - 36.87^\\circ \\approx 53.13^\\circ')} /></li>
      <li>Area = (3 × 4) / 2 = <strong>6 cm²</strong>; perimeter = 3 + 4 + 5 = <strong>12 cm</strong></li>
      <li>Altitude <em>h</em> = (3 × 4) / 5 = <strong>2.4 cm</strong>; inradius <em>r</em> = (3 + 4 − 5) / 2 = <strong>1 cm</strong>; circumradius <em>R</em> = 5 / 2 = <strong>2.5 cm</strong></li>
    </ol>
    <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-on-surface-variant"><strong>Check:</strong> 3² + 4² = 9 + 16 = 25 = 5², and 36.87° + 53.13° = 90°. The hypotenuse 5 exceeds both legs.</p>
    <button type="button" class="btn-secondary mt-3 text-sm" data-try-calc data-a="3" data-b="4">Try in Calculator: a = 3, b = 4</button>
  </article>

  <article class="not-prose my-6 rounded-xl border border-outline-variant/25 bg-surface-container-lowest/60 p-5">
    <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold">Example 2: Find a Missing Leg</h3>
    <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-on-surface-variant"><strong>Given:</strong> <em>c</em> = 13 cm, <em>b</em> = 12 cm. <strong>Find:</strong> <em>a</em> and the remaining measurements.</p>
    <img src="/images/hub/hub-ex2-51213.svg" alt="5-12-13 right triangle with hypotenuse 13 and leg 12 labeled" class="mx-auto my-4 h-auto w-full max-w-sm" loading="lazy" />
    <ol class="list-decimal space-y-1 pl-5 text-sm">
      <li>Formula: <span class="math-textbook" set:html={tex('a = \\sqrt{c^2 - b^2}')} /></li>
      <li>Substitution: <span class="math-textbook" set:html={tex('a = \\sqrt{13^2 - 12^2}')} /></li>
      <li>Simplify: <span class="math-textbook" set:html={tex('a = \\sqrt{169 - 144} = \\sqrt{25}')} /></li>
      <li>Answer: <strong><em>a</em> = 5 cm</strong></li>
      <li>Angles: <span class="math-textbook" set:html={tex('A = \\arcsin(5/13) \\approx 22.62^\\circ')} />, <span class="math-textbook" set:html={tex('B \\approx 67.38^\\circ')} /></li>
      <li>Area = (5 × 12) / 2 = <strong>30 cm²</strong>; perimeter = 5 + 12 + 13 = <strong>30 cm</strong></li>
    </ol>
    <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-on-surface-variant"><strong>Why the hypotenuse must be longest:</strong> the subtraction 169 − 144 only works because 13 &gt; 12. If the known leg exceeded the supposed hypotenuse, the value under the root would be negative and no real triangle could exist.</p>
    <button type="button" class="btn-secondary mt-3 text-sm" data-try-calc data-c="13" data-b="12">Try in Calculator: c = 13, b = 12</button>
  </article>

  <article class="not-prose my-6 rounded-xl border border-outline-variant/25 bg-surface-container-lowest/60 p-5">
    <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold">Example 3: Solve Using an Angle and the Hypotenuse</h3>
    <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-on-surface-variant"><strong>Given:</strong> <em>c</em> = 10 cm, <em>A</em> = 30°. <strong>Find:</strong> <em>a</em>, <em>b</em>, <em>B</em>.</p>
    <img src="/images/hub/hub-ex3-1030.svg" alt="Right triangle with hypotenuse 10, angle A 30 degrees, angle B 60 degrees" class="mx-auto my-4 h-auto w-full max-w-sm" loading="lazy" />
    <ol class="list-decimal space-y-1 pl-5 text-sm">
      <li>Opposite leg uses sine: <span class="math-textbook" set:html={tex('a = c \\times \\sin(A) = 10 \\times \\sin(30^\\circ) = 10 \\times 0.5')} /> → <strong><em>a</em> = 5 cm</strong></li>
      <li>Adjacent leg uses cosine: <span class="math-textbook" set:html={tex('b = c \\times \\cos(A) = 10 \\times \\cos(30^\\circ) \\approx 10 \\times 0.86603')} /> → <strong><em>b</em> ≈ 8.6603 cm</strong></li>
      <li>Remaining angle: <span class="math-textbook" set:html={tex('B = 90^\\circ - 30^\\circ = 60^\\circ')} /></li>
      <li>Area = (5 × 8.6603) / 2 ≈ <strong>21.6506 cm²</strong></li>
    </ol>
    <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-on-surface-variant"><strong>Why sine for <em>a</em>?</strong> Sine is defined as opposite over hypotenuse, and <em>a</em> is opposite angle <em>A</em>, so sin(30°) = <em>a</em>/10 directly isolates <em>a</em>. Cosine is adjacent over hypotenuse, matching <em>b</em>. Choosing the ratio whose numerator is the wanted side avoids extra algebra.</p>
    <button type="button" class="btn-secondary mt-3 text-sm" data-try-calc data-c="10" data-A="30">Try in Calculator: c = 10, A = 30°</button>
  </article>

  <article class="not-prose my-6 rounded-xl border border-outline-variant/25 bg-surface-container-lowest/60 p-5">
    <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold">Example 4: Find an Angle from Two Legs</h3>
    <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-on-surface-variant"><strong>Given:</strong> <em>a</em> = 8 m, <em>b</em> = 15 m. <strong>Find:</strong> <em>A</em>, <em>B</em>, <em>c</em>.</p>
    <img src="/images/hub/hub-ex4-81517.svg" alt="8-15-17 right triangle with legs 8 and 15 meters" class="mx-auto my-4 h-auto w-full max-w-sm" loading="lazy" />
    <ol class="list-decimal space-y-1 pl-5 text-sm">
      <li>Formula: <span class="math-textbook" set:html={tex('\\tan(A) = \\frac{a}{b} = \\frac{8}{15}')} /></li>
      <li>Inverse tangent: <span class="math-textbook" set:html={tex('A = \\arctan(8/15) \\approx 28.07^\\circ')} /></li>
      <li>Complement: <span class="math-textbook" set:html={tex('B = 90^\\circ - 28.07^\\circ \\approx 61.93^\\circ')} /></li>
      <li>Hypotenuse: <span class="math-textbook" set:html={tex('c = \\sqrt{8^2 + 15^2} = \\sqrt{64 + 225} = \\sqrt{289} = 17')} /> m</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-on-surface-variant"><strong>About arctan:</strong> tangent maps an angle to a ratio; arctan reverses the mapping, turning the ratio 8/15 back into the angle 28.07°. It is the right inverse when you know both legs, because tan(A) = opposite/adjacent uses exactly those two sides.</p>
    <button type="button" class="btn-secondary mt-3 text-sm" data-try-calc data-a="8" data-b="15">Try in Calculator: a = 8, b = 15</button>
  </article>

  <article class="not-prose my-6 rounded-xl border border-outline-variant/25 bg-surface-container-lowest/60 p-5">
    <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold">Example 5: Find Missing Sides from Area and One Leg</h3>
    <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-on-surface-variant"><strong>Given:</strong> area <em>K</em> = 24 cm², leg <em>a</em> = 6 cm. <strong>Find:</strong> <em>b</em>, <em>c</em>. (Manual calculation: this solver accepts side and angle inputs, so area-first problems start here and finish in the <a href="/right-triangle-area-calculator/">Area Calculator</a>.)</p>
    <img src="/images/hub/hub-ex5-6810.svg" alt="6-8-10 right triangle derived from area 24 and leg 6" class="mx-auto my-4 h-auto w-full max-w-sm" loading="lazy" />
    <ol class="list-decimal space-y-1 pl-5 text-sm">
      <li>Area formula: <span class="math-textbook" set:html={tex('K = \\frac{a \\times b}{2}')} /></li>
      <li>Solve for <em>b</em>: <span class="math-textbook" set:html={tex('b = \\frac{2K}{a} = \\frac{48}{6} = 8')} /> cm</li>
      <li>Hypotenuse: <span class="math-textbook" set:html={tex('c = \\sqrt{6^2 + 8^2} = \\sqrt{36 + 64} = \\sqrt{100} = 10')} /> cm</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-on-surface-variant"><strong>The idea:</strong> area ties the two legs together in one equation, so knowing the area and one leg determines the other leg. Once both legs are known, the problem reduces to Example 1.</p>
    <a href="/right-triangle-area-calculator/" class="btn-secondary mt-3 inline-block text-sm">Open the Area Calculator</a>
  </article>
</section>

<section id="hub-real-world" class="mx-auto mt-8 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Real-World Applications of Right Triangle Calculations</h2>
  <p>Five scenarios where the same triangle mathematics solves a concrete problem. Each states its simplifying assumptions honestly.</p>

  <article class="not-prose my-6 rounded-xl border border-outline-variant/25 bg-surface-container-lowest/60 p-5">
    <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold">Ladder Against a Wall</h3>
    <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-on-surface-variant">A 5 m ladder leans against a vertical wall with its base 3 m from the wall. How high does it reach?</p>
    <img src="/images/hub/hub-prac1-ladder.svg" alt="Ladder of 5 meters leaning against a wall, base 3 meters out, reaching 4 meters high" class="mx-auto my-4 h-auto w-full max-w-md" loading="lazy" />
    <ol class="list-decimal space-y-1 pl-5 text-sm">
      <li>The wall, ground, and ladder form a right triangle with hypotenuse 5 m and one leg 3 m.</li>
      <li><span class="math-textbook" set:html={tex('h = \\sqrt{5^2 - 3^2} = \\sqrt{25 - 9} = \\sqrt{16}')} /></li>
      <li><strong>Answer: 4 m.</strong> The ladder reaches 4 meters up the wall.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-on-surface-variant"><strong>Assumptions:</strong> perfectly vertical wall and level ground. This is a geometry calculation, not a ladder-safety recommendation.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="not-prose my-6 rounded-xl border border-outline-variant/25 bg-surface-container-lowest/60 p-5">
    <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold">Wheelchair Ramp Geometry</h3>
    <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-on-surface-variant">A ramp rises 0.75 m over a horizontal run of 6 m. Find the ramp length, incline angle, and slope.</p>
    <img src="/images/hub/hub-prac2-ramp.svg" alt="Side view of a ramp with 0.75 meter rise and 6 meter run, incline angle 7.13 degrees" class="mx-auto my-4 h-auto w-full max-w-md" loading="lazy" />
    <ol class="list-decimal space-y-1 pl-5 text-sm">
      <li>Ramp length: <span class="math-textbook" set:html={tex('L = \\sqrt{6^2 + 0.75^2} = \\sqrt{36.5625} \\approx 6.047')} /> m</li>
      <li>Incline angle: <span class="math-textbook" set:html={tex('\\theta = \\arctan(0.75/6) = \\arctan(0.125) \\approx 7.13^\\circ')} /></li>
      <li>Slope: 0.75 / 6 = 0.125 = <strong>12.5%</strong></li>
    </ol>
    <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-on-surface-variant"><strong>Assumptions:</strong> straight ramp with constant slope. This is a mathematical example only, not a determination of building-code or accessibility compliance.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="not-prose my-6 rounded-xl border border-outline-variant/25 bg-surface-container-lowest/60 p-5">
    <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold">Roof Pitch and Rafter Length</h3>
    <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-on-surface-variant">A roof has a 6:12 pitch (6 ft of rise per 12 ft of run). For a 12 ft horizontal run, find the rafter length and roof angle.</p>
    <img src="/images/hub/hub-prac3-roof.svg" alt="Roof cross-section with 6 foot rise, 12 foot run, rafter 13.42 feet, angle 26.57 degrees" class="mx-auto my-4 h-auto w-full max-w-md" loading="lazy" />
    <ol class="list-decimal space-y-1 pl-5 text-sm">
      <li>Rise = 6 ft for the 12 ft run (the 6:12 ratio).</li>
      <li>Rafter: <span class="math-textbook" set:html={tex('R = \\sqrt{12^2 + 6^2} = \\sqrt{180} \\approx 13.42')} /> ft</li>
      <li>Roof angle: <span class="math-textbook" set:html={tex('\\theta = \\arctan(6/12) = \\arctan(0.5) \\approx 26.57^\\circ')} /></li>
    </ol>
    <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-on-surface-variant"><strong>Assumptions:</strong> overhangs, ridge connections, and construction allowances are excluded; the rafter is the straight-line hypotenuse of the pitch triangle.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="not-prose my-6 rounded-xl border border-outline-variant/25 bg-surface-container-lowest/60 p-5">
    <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold">Diagonal of a Rectangular Room</h3>
    <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-on-surface-variant">A room measures 8 m by 15 m. What is the diagonal distance between opposite corners?</p>
    <img src="/images/hub/hub-prac4-room.svg" alt="Top-down view of an 8 by 15 meter room with a 17 meter diagonal" class="mx-auto my-4 h-auto w-full max-w-md" loading="lazy" />
    <ol class="list-decimal space-y-1 pl-5 text-sm">
      <li>A rectangle's diagonal splits it into two congruent right triangles with legs 8 m and 15 m.</li>
      <li><span class="math-textbook" set:html={tex('d = \\sqrt{8^2 + 15^2} = \\sqrt{64 + 225} = \\sqrt{289}')} /></li>
      <li><strong>Answer: 17 m.</strong> Recognizing the 8-15-17 triple gives the answer without a calculator.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-on-surface-variant"><strong>Assumptions:</strong> the room is a true rectangle with square corners.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="not-prose my-6 rounded-xl border border-outline-variant/25 bg-surface-container-lowest/60 p-5">
    <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold">The 3-4-5 Squaring Method</h3>
    <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Builders check a corner for squareness by measuring 3 units along one side, 4 along the other, and confirming the diagonal is 5 units.</p>
    <img src="/images/hub/hub-prac5-squaring.svg" alt="3-4-5 triangle and scaled 6-8-10 triangle used to check square corners on a construction site" class="mx-auto my-4 h-auto w-full max-w-md" loading="lazy" />
    <ol class="list-decimal space-y-1 pl-5 text-sm">
      <li>Check: <span class="math-textbook" set:html={tex('3^2 + 4^2 = 9 + 16 = 25 = 5^2')} />. The converse of the Pythagorean theorem says a triangle satisfying this equation must contain a right angle.</li>
      <li>Scaling up keeps the angle: <span class="math-textbook" set:html={tex('6^2 + 8^2 = 36 + 64 = 100 = 10^2')} />, so a 6-8-10 triangle is also exactly square.</li>
      <li>Any consistent scale works (9-12-15, 30-40-50) because multiplying all sides by the same factor preserves the <span class="math-textbook" set:html={tex('a^2+b^2=c^2')} /> relationship.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-on-surface-variant"><strong>Assumptions:</strong> measurements are taken carefully in matching units. This field check does not replace professional surveying for structural work.</p>
  </article>
</section>

<section id="hub-special" class="mx-auto mt-8 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Special Right Triangles and Their Ratios</h2>
  <p>Two triangles appear so often that their side ratios are worth memorizing. Both follow directly from the Pythagorean theorem.</p>

  <h3>45°–45°–90° Triangle</h3>
  <div class="not-prose my-4 grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2 sm:items-center">
    <img src="/images/hub/hub-special-454590.svg" alt="45-45-90 triangle with equal legs of 7 cm and hypotenuse 7 root 2" class="h-auto w-full" loading="lazy" />
    <div>
      <p class="text-sm">The two legs are equal and both acute angles are 45°, giving the side ratio <span class="math-textbook" set:html={tex('1 : 1 : \\sqrt{2}')} />. If each leg is 7 cm, the hypotenuse is <span class="math-textbook" set:html={tex('7\\sqrt{2} \\approx 9.90')} /> cm.</p>
      <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Cut any square along its diagonal and you get two of these triangles, which is why the ratio appears in paper sizes, screen diagonals, and miter cuts.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3>30°–60°–90° Triangle</h3>
  <div class="not-prose my-4 grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2 sm:items-center">
    <img src="/images/hub/hub-special-306090.svg" alt="30-60-90 triangle with sides 5, 5 root 3, and 10" class="h-auto w-full" loading="lazy" />
    <div>
      <p class="text-sm">The side ratio is <span class="math-textbook" set:html={tex('1 : \\sqrt{3} : 2')} />: the shortest side (opposite 30°) is half the hypotenuse, and the longer leg is <span class="math-textbook" set:html={tex('\\sqrt{3}')} /> times the shortest. With short leg 5 cm: long leg = <span class="math-textbook" set:html={tex('5\\sqrt{3} \\approx 8.66')} /> cm, hypotenuse = 10 cm.</p>
      <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-on-surface-variant">This triangle is half of an equilateral triangle, which is why the 1:2 relationship between the short leg and hypotenuse is exact.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
