<section id="hub-mistakes" class="mx-auto mt-8 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Common Right Triangle Calculation Mistakes</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Treating a leg as the hypotenuse.</strong> The hypotenuse is the side opposite the right angle, the only side that touches neither endpoint of the right-angle marker. In the 3-4-5 triangle, 5 is the hypotenuse; computing <span class="math-textbook" set:html={tex('\\sqrt{5^2 + 4^2}')} /> instead of <span class="math-textbook" set:html={tex('\\sqrt{5^2 - 4^2}')} /> answers a different question.</li>
    <li><strong>Using the wrong trigonometric ratio.</strong> With angle <em>A</em> = 30° and hypotenuse 10, the side opposite <em>A</em> needs sine (<em>a</em> = 10 sin 30° = 5). Using cosine by mistake computes the adjacent leg instead (8.66). Always name the wanted side as opposite or adjacent <em>relative to your angle</em> before choosing.</li>
    <li><strong>Incorrect square-root grouping.</strong> <span class="math-textbook" set:html={tex('c = \\sqrt{a^2 + b^2}')} /> adds first, then takes the root: √(9 + 16) = 5. The misreading √a² + b² takes the root of <em>a</em>² alone: 3 + 16 = 19, which is wrong. Parentheses under the radical are not optional.</li>
    <li><strong>Entering inconsistent measurements.</strong> <em>c</em> = 5 with <em>a</em> = 6 contradicts the hypotenuse-longest rule, and <em>A</em> = 40° with <em>B</em> = 60° contradicts <em>A</em> + <em>B</em> = 90°. The calculator rejects these; fix the value that was measured or copied incorrectly.</li>
    <li><strong>Mixing degrees and radians.</strong> This calculator works in degrees. Entering 0.524 (radians for 30°) where 30 was meant shrinks every trig result. Convert first: degrees = radians × 180/π.</li>
    <li><strong>Rounding too early.</strong> Computing with <em>b</em> ≈ 8.66 instead of 8.660254 and then squaring compounds the error: 8.66² = 74.9956 versus the true 75. Keep full precision through intermediate steps and round only the final display.</li>
    <li><strong>Mixing length units.</strong> A leg of 3 meters with a hypotenuse of 500 centimeters is really 3 m and 5 m, a valid 3-4-5 triangle. Entered as 3 and 500 in one unit, the same inputs look impossible. Convert to one unit before calculating.</li>
  </ol>
</section>

<section id="hub-verify" class="mx-auto mt-8 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Verify a Right Triangle Solution</h2>
  <p>Run these eight checks on any solved triangle. The first six are geometric identities; the last two are sanity checks on units and signs.</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Pythagoras:</strong> <span class="math-textbook" set:html={tex('a^2 + b^2')} /> equals <span class="math-textbook" set:html={tex('c^2')} /> within rounding tolerance (for example, agree to at least 4 decimal places).</li>
    <li><strong>Angle sum:</strong> <em>A</em> + <em>B</em> = 90° before display rounding is applied.</li>
    <li><strong>Longest side:</strong> <em>c</em> &gt; <em>a</em> and <em>c</em> &gt; <em>b</em>, strictly.</li>
    <li><strong>Area consistency:</strong> <span class="math-textbook" set:html={tex('K = a \\times b / 2')} /> matches the reported area.</li>
    <li><strong>Altitude consistency:</strong> <span class="math-textbook" set:html={tex('h \\times c / 2')} /> gives the same area, since the altitude and hypotenuse are another base-height pair.</li>
    <li><strong>Projection sum:</strong> <em>p</em> + <em>q</em> = <em>c</em> exactly (up to rounding).</li>
    <li><strong>Radii positive:</strong> <em>r</em> &gt; 0 and <em>R</em> &gt; 0, with <em>R</em> = <em>c</em>/2.</li>
    <li><strong>Correct dimensions:</strong> sides, altitude, projections, and radii are lengths; area is length²; angles are in degrees.</li>
  </ol>
  <p class="text-sm text-on-surface-variant">
    <strong>How calculations are performed:</strong> the solver uses double-precision floating-point arithmetic
    (about 15–16 significant decimal digits). Trigonometric functions take angles in degrees and convert
    internally. Displayed values are rounded for readability, but every check above should be evaluated on the
    unrounded values, where agreement holds to roughly 10 decimal places.
  </p>
</section>

